  "title": "Felling: both of your two issues, together",
  "question": "Does either read as a tree hinging at the trunk and going over with its leaves still on it? And if it is still wrong, is it the motion or the pile it leaves?",
  "context_md": "**Your two issues from the last card, both acted on.**\n\n*\"It should hinge at the trunk... not just unzip and fall to the ground.\"* The crown was being cut into **56 separate pieces at the instant of the cut**, before a cell had moved, so there was never a tree to hinge. Every piece now shares one hinge on the stump: a piece high up travels several times faster than one near the base, so it sweeps over as one, the base lands first, and the hinge releases on impact.\n\n*\"All the leaves still fall off the branch.\"* The rule that was meant to hold them only looked at the four cells immediately touching a leaf, and a crown is mostly leaf resting on other leaves — so it held **36%** of the foliage and let the rest go. It now reaches one cell further, which holds **74%**. The foliage that used to run five or more cells clear of any wood dropped from 214 cells to 74.\n\nThat also gave back what the hinge had cost: swinging makes it land harder, and wood surviving as solid pieces had fallen from 732 cells to 587 — with the leaves holding on it is **772**, better than either.\n\n**What I still cannot judge and you can:** whether the settled pile reads as branches broken on the ground or just as a tidier heap. It is more compact than before, which is not the same thing as more like wood.",
